R&C patch 7064: PAD_ANCHOR_CLOCK=flips[:N] -- the anchored part of a pad script on a frame clock (ToD regression gate).
DRAFT 2026-11-11, written without a build or a run (machine reserved): run port/rc/tests/run_pad_anchor_test.sh (new
section 5) and a Windows build before relying on it. Default off: without the env var the player is 7062's.

What: with PAD_ANCHOR_CLOCK=flips the events after the script's "@fs:TEXT" anchor (7062) count guest flips since the
anchor resolved (cellGcm_flip_request_count, read once per pad poll), 60 flips = 1 s of script time ("flips:N" sets N).
Holds of anchored events ("for D s") count flips the same way; the 40-poll press of an event without a duration is
unchanged. Events before the anchor stay on the pad clock (wall seconds, or flips under PAD_CLOCK=flips). Logs:
"[cellPad] PAD_SCRIPT: anchored events on the flip clock, N flips = 1 s (PAD_ANCHOR_CLOCK)" at load, "[cellPad] script
anchor at flip F: ..." when it fires; fired anchored events print t=@f+T. The last-fired stick event wins by fire order
(a sequence number) instead of fired_at, since fired_at is now on each event's own clock.

Why: tools/pad/tods-newgame-anchored.txt's in-level inputs are timed in wall seconds after Metropolis' control start, so
at 35 fps an input lands at a different game frame than at 47 fps. In the scripted ToD runs Ratchet dies and respawns
in Metropolis a number of times that varies with fps (deaths cluster at the "jump, then walk right" part of the cycle):
tods-anch-main7062-2 and tods-anch-br7062-2 (same script) had 2 and 4 respawns in the gate's 140 s window. On a frame
clock the same input lands at the same flip after control start whatever the fps.
Not 3006/#344's PAD_CLOCK=flips: that clocks the WHOLE script in raw flips (the script's numbers would have to be
rewritten in flips, and the menu part would follow the logo and loading screens' flip rates); 7064 keeps the script in
seconds and changes only the anchored part, so the same script file runs on either clock and an A/B is one env var.
ASSUMPTION: the game steps its simulation per frame. If ToD integrates a measured frame time instead, the same input at
the same flip still finds Ratchet in a different place at a different fps, and only an fps-independent result shows
the clock helps; the A/B below decides it.

A/B (later, when a run is booked; one GPU run at a time, same exe, same window):
  build: tools/ps3recomp-patched.sh, then the ToD Windows build (exe in work/stage/tods), 7062 + 7064 in the patch set;
  run_pad_anchor_test.sh first (sections 1-5 pass).
  arm W (wall):  tools/tod-regression.sh tods-fclk-wall-1
  arm F (flips): EXTRA_ENV=PAD_ANCHOR_CLOCK=flips tools/tod-regression.sh tods-fclk-flips-1
  (the log must show "anchored events on the flip clock"); then each arm
  again with a second exe of a different fps (e.g. the SSE4.1 branch) or under load. Compare, per arm, the flip numbers
  (and anchor-relative flips) of each "spu1 stopped by RunCntl" respawn: F should put them at the same anchor-relative
  flip in every run and exe, W should not. Both arms must PASS the per-event gate. Do not retime
  tods-newgame-anchored.txt until F has shown it.
2026-11-11 (code review of rc-int-1600): a script without an anchor ignored PAD_ANCHOR_CLOCK silently, so an F arm with
such a script ran on the wall clock with no line saying so; it now prints "WARNING PAD_ANCHOR_CLOCK='...' ignored: the
script has no @fs: anchor, wall clock kept" (tools/tod-regression.sh already FAILs a PAD_ANCHOR_CLOCK arm without the
flip-clock line).
